CompCert4Lean licensing

We license the project's original, independently reusable work under the MIT
License (LICENSES/MIT.txt). This includes our Python drivers, test runners,
codec generator, build configuration and documentation. Where a file incorporates
upstream material, that material and adaptations of it keep the terms below.
License notices beside individual source files identify the translated modules
and their origins. The combined compiler includes passes covered by the INRIA Non-Commercial License
Agreement. Section 4 requires distribution of the port of those passes on the
same terms. Commercial use of the combined compiler requires separate permission.

File guide (Lean paths below are relative to CompCert4Lean/)

* CompCert 3.17, INRIA Non-Commercial License Agreement. This covers the
  translations in Backend/, Cfrontend/, Driver/ and X86/, except for the
  dual-licensed modules listed below. It also covers the allocator adaptations
  of Regalloc.ml, IRC.ml, Splitting.ml and the x86 target rules in Allocator/
  (the source mapping is in Allocator/README.md). The independently written
  IndexSetCache.lean and the allocator README are MIT.

* CompCert 3.17, choice of LGPL-2.1-or-later or the INRIA agreement. This
  covers translations of upstream common/ and lib/, Backend/Cminor.lean,
  Cfrontend/{Clight,Cop,Csem,Cstrategy,Csyntax,Ctypes,Ctyping}.lean,
  X86/Builtins1.lean and X86_64/Archi.lean. Common/Events/Core.lean comes from
  common/Events.v. The independently written Common/ExternalCalls.lean and
  Lib/Floats/Sqrt.lean are MIT. For the Coq-derived files in Lib/, see below.

* Flocq 4.2.1, LGPL-3.0-or-later. This covers Flocq/, the Lean translation
  of the floating-point library bundled with CompCert 3.17. The adjacent
  .license files identify the upstream sources and their authors.

* Coq 8.20.1 standard library, LGPL-2.1. This covers the translated
  standard-library material in Foundation/{Logic,Numbers,Ordered,Recursion,
  RocqString,FlocqReal}.lean, Lib/{FSetDecide,FSetFacts,FSetInterface,
  FSetProperties,MSetAVL,MSetGenTree}.lean and Lib/Floats/Model.lean.
  Foundation/RocqStdlib.lean is the project's MIT-licensed import list.

* Integration code. The project's own configuration, interchange code and
  adapters are MIT. Prototype/RuntimeHelpers.lean includes switch compilation
  from common/Switchaux.ml (dual-licensed as above) and heuristics from
  backend/Selectionaux.ml and backend/Linearizeaux.ml (INRIA agreement).
  Prototype/AllocationReport.lean and Prototype/AllocationTrace.lean replay the
  CompCert-derived pipeline or allocator. Those adaptations carry the INRIA
  terms. In Prototype/Codecs.lean and bridge/codecs.ml, the codec machinery is
  project work and the generated constructor tables follow their source
  interfaces: the CompCert C and common interfaces are dual-licensed, and
  x86/Asm.v carries the INRIA terms. The same source rule applies to x86
  builtin patterns adapted in bridge/bridge.ml. The linked upstream frontend,
  printer and runtime retain their respective licenses.

* Tests. Imported files under tests/small-tests/ are identified, by path and
  hash, in tests/small-tests/provenance.json. Their default is GPL-2.0-or-later,
  subject to individual notices, including those in spass/. See the corpus
  README.md and LICENSE. Our SUITE.md, manifest.json and provenance.json are
  MIT. Our other test harnesses and original checks are also MIT. If a check
  reproduces part of a ported implementation, that portion retains its source
  terms (for example, allocator reference routines in tests/allocator/Main.lean
  and tests/optimization/).

Upstream copyright and license texts

CompCert is copyright INRIA and AbsInt Angewandte Informatik GmbH. The complete
license from the pinned CompCert 3.17 checkout, including the INRIA agreement (also in LICENSES/LicenseRef-INRIA-CompCert.txt),
LGPL 2.1, LGPL 3 and the BSD 3-Clause runtime notice, is in
LICENSES/CompCert-3.17.txt. LICENSES/GPL-3.0.txt supplies the underlying GPL
text for LGPL 3. The Coq library is copyright INRIA, CNRS and contributors.
See the notices accompanying the translated libraries and the original sources:

